WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.340
Why would a cell copy its DNA in one place, and divide in another?

2
00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:11.800
Nineteenth-century anatomists saw nuclei at many heights.

3
00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:14.660
They read the wall as several kinds of cells.

4
00:00:15.683 --> 00:00:21.203
In 1935, F. C. Sauer argued for a simpler picture.

5
00:00:22.183 --> 00:00:25.203
One kind of long cell, whose nucleus moves inside it.

6
00:00:26.017 --> 00:00:31.077
A single layer of cells with nuclei at many heights is called a pseudostratified

7
00:00:31.077 --> 00:00:32.077
epithelium.

8
00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:35.220
Picture a lift, with the ventricle at the bottom.

9
00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:37.100
The shaft is the cell.

10
00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:39.020
The car is the nucleus.

11
00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:42.210
The shaft runs all the way up to the pia.

12
00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:46.570
But the car only serves the lowest floors, the ventricular zone.

13
00:00:47.633 --> 00:00:49.533
In G1, the car rides up.

14
00:00:50.313 --> 00:00:54.833
It stops near the top of the ventricular zone for S phase, when DNA is copied.

15
00:00:55.933 --> 00:01:00.173
In G2, it comes back down to the ground floor, the apical surface.

16
00:01:01.073 --> 00:01:02.213
The cell divides there.

17
00:01:03.133 --> 00:01:05.393
Each cell's car runs on its own timetable.

18
00:01:06.393 --> 00:01:08.233
That is why nuclei sit at every height.

19
00:01:09.217 --> 00:01:12.517
This movement is called interkinetic nuclear migration.

20
00:01:13.797 --> 00:01:14.797
Movement between divisions.

21
00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:18.360
A pulse label made Sauer's cycle a timetable.

22
00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:24.600
Tritiated thymidine, a radioactive DNA building block, is taken up only in S phase.

23
00:01:25.683 --> 00:01:28.603
Give a brief pulse, then fix the tissue at intervals.

24
00:01:29.623 --> 00:01:32.063
Soon after the pulse, where are the labeled nuclei?

25
00:01:32.883 --> 00:01:34.663
And when do labeled mitoses appear?

26
00:01:41.167 --> 00:01:45.427
Soon after the pulse, labeled nuclei sit out in the ventricular zone.

27
00:01:46.287 --> 00:01:48.027
No cell in mitosis is labeled.

28
00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:53.100
Later, the labeled nuclei come down and divide at the ventricle.

29
00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:56.980
In 1959, two groups reported this.

30
00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:00.480
Labeled nuclei moved in step with the cell cycle.

31
00:02:01.483 --> 00:02:07.063
In eleven-day mouse embryos, no labeled mitosis appeared until an hour after the

32
00:02:07.063 --> 00:02:12.003
injection. That lag roughly spans G2, which includes the trip back down.

33
00:02:12.967 --> 00:02:14.827
So why divide at the ventricle?

34
00:02:15.527 --> 00:02:16.947
The answer is partly settled.

35
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:19.420
The best-supported part.

36
00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:24.980
For much of interphase, the centrosome sits at the apical surface, at the base of

37
00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:25.980
the primary cilium.

38
00:02:26.883 --> 00:02:29.983
A dividing cell builds its spindle from its centrosomes.

39
00:02:30.843 --> 00:02:33.843
On the usual account, the nucleus travels to meet them.

40
00:02:34.663 --> 00:02:38.363
Late in G2, the centrosome can also move to meet the nucleus.

41
00:02:39.367 --> 00:02:40.927
One proposal is packing.

42
00:02:41.887 --> 00:02:44.307
Only a cell in mitosis is round and wide.

43
00:02:45.167 --> 00:02:46.787
The rest can stay thin at the surface.

44
00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:51.470
A round cell takes about twenty times the room of a thin foot.

45
00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:56.110
So on average, each cell needs about a tenth of a dividing cell's room.

46
00:02:57.083 --> 00:03:00.763
Staggered timetables let more progenitors share the ventricle.

47
00:03:01.667 --> 00:03:03.467
A second proposal is signals.

48
00:03:04.407 --> 00:03:08.927
In zebrafish retina, Notch signalling was reported to fall from apical to basal.

49
00:03:09.907 --> 00:03:13.207
So how far a nucleus travels could change the signal it meets.

50
00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:18.600
Now the payoff. Apical radial glia divide at the surface.

51
00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:22.280
So where a cell divides is a first clue to what kind it is.

52
00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:28.100
Most mitoses at the ventricle are apical radial glia, though some intermediate

53
00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:29.700
progenitors divide there too.

54
00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:35.420
Mitoses away from it are mostly intermediate progenitors, or outer radial glia.

55
00:03:36.383 --> 00:03:38.643
To name the cell, add its shape and markers.

56
00:03:39.623 --> 00:03:43.883
To learn what its daughters become, you need lineage, the descendants of one cell.
